// A bare `import { + }` is refused by the grammar itself: the `import_name`
// production recognises the operator spelling and then `Refuse`s it, so the
// message says what to write (`wrapping_add as +`) rather than "expected an
// import name, found `+`". This pins that `+` reaches that refusal — a grammar
// that dropped `+` from its `operator` production would fall through to the
// generic parse error instead.
import { + } from builtins;

fn main() {}
--- errors ---
error: the "+" operator has no bare form; pick a semantics and import it aliased, e.g. `wrapping_add as +` or `saturating_add as +` from builtins
 --> ./tests/cases/imports/err_bare_plus_operator.aipl:7:10
  |
7 | import { + } from builtins;
  |          ^
